Bootstrap: docker
From: ubuntu:22.04

# Standalone external tool, NOT a `pyproject.toml` extra of this project:
# bakes in Zhang-Ran-0119/DeepPSA (https://github.com/Zhang-Ran-0119/DeepPSA),
# a geometric-deep-learning PROTAC synthetic-accessibility scorer, for use as
# one more external synthesizability score alongside the SA/SC/RA/SYBA/GASA/FS
# stack in retro_scores/. Unlike inference.def/training.def/scoring.def, this
# image has no %files section -- nothing here depends on
# retrotac/scripts/config, so apptainer/bind_live_repo.sh is not needed (and
# does nothing useful) for this container. See apptainer/README.md for the
# general five-decision recipe the other three follow; this file deviates
# from decisions 2/3 (uv + `--extra <name>`) for the reason below.
#
# DeepPSA's own environment.yaml is a full `conda env export`, not a plain
# pip requirements list: several of its pins (openbabel, boost, oddt, dgl
# with CUDA-specific build strings) are conda-only builds with no clean pip
# wheel on Linux, so this replays that file via mamba rather than
# hand-translating it to uv/pip -- same reasoning as scoring.def's
# venv-scoring half (retro_scores/README.md: "a flat pip install -r does not
# solve"), just via conda instead of staged pip pins since DeepPSA ships a
# conda export, not a pip one.
#
# DeepPSA's Academic Free Licence permits academic use only, not unauthorised
# commercial use -- see /opt/deeppsa/LICENSE inside the built image.

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        ca-certificates curl git git-lfs build-essential libxrender1 libxext6 libsm6
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    # ---- mamba (Miniforge3) -- parallel to how uv is curl-installed in the
    # other three .def files, but this stack needs conda's own package
    # builds (openbabel, boost, dgl, ...), not just a Python interpreter +
    # pip wheels.
    curl -L -o /tmp/miniforge.sh \
        https://github.com/conda-forge/miniforge/releases/latest/download/Miniforge3-Linux-x86_64.sh
    bash /tmp/miniforge.sh -b -p /opt/miniforge3
    rm -f /tmp/miniforge.sh
    export PATH="/opt/miniforge3/bin:${PATH}"

    # ---- clone DeepPSA. A plain `git clone` (unlike `pip install git+URL`,
    # see apptainer/README.md's "1d") DOES smudge Git LFS content correctly,
    # so this pulls the real checkpoint weights, not the ~130-byte pointer
    # stubs the repo shows over a non-LFS-aware clone.
    git lfs install --skip-repo
    git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/Zhang-Ran-0119/DeepPSA.git /opt/deeppsa

    # Fail loudly at build time, not at first inference, if LFS didn't
    # smudge (e.g. an LFS bandwidth/quota hiccup) -- a real checkpoint is
    # tens of MB, the pointer stub is ~130 bytes.
    ckpt_size=$(stat -c%s /opt/deeppsa/PSA/FinetuneModel.pth)
    if [ "$ckpt_size" -lt 10000 ]; then
        echo "PSA/FinetuneModel.pth is only ${ckpt_size} bytes -- still an LFS pointer stub, not the real checkpoint" >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    # Upstream bug in Zhang-Ran-0119/DeepPSA (confirmed by actually running
    # PSA.py on a CPU-only host, not from the clone succeeding): its
    # torch.load('PSA/FinetuneModel.pth') call passes no map_location, so it
    # always tries to deserialize the checkpoint (saved from CUDA tensors)
    # onto a CUDA device and crashes outright -- even though the very next
    # lines already compute the right `device` (cuda if available, else cpu)
    # and use it for everything else. One-line patch: route torch.load
    # through that same `device` variable, so CPU-only hosts work too (same
    # shape of fix as GASA's errno patch in scoring.def).
    sed -i "s|torch.load('PSA/FinetuneModel.pth')|torch.load('PSA/FinetuneModel.pth', map_location=device)|" \
        /opt/deeppsa/PSA.py
    grep -q "map_location=device" /opt/deeppsa/PSA.py || { echo "PSA.py map_location patch did not apply" >&2; exit 1; }

    # ---- replay DeepPSA's own pinned conda env at an explicit prefix, kept
    # OUTSIDE /opt/deeppsa so a future live-dev bind-mount over /opt/deeppsa
    # (were one ever set up) can't bury it -- same principle as
    # apptainer/README.md's "4" (venv lives outside the code it serves).
    # CONDA_OVERRIDE_CUDA: the build runs with no GPU visible (--fakeroot
    # doesn't pass --nv), so mamba's solver can't detect the __cuda virtual
    # package that environment.yaml's cudatoolkit=11.8/pytorch/tensorflow
    # builds require and fails with "invalid section(s)"-unrelated but
    # equally opaque "requires __cuda, which is missing on the system"
    # errors. This tells the solver to assume CUDA 11.8 is present, same
    # spirit as this repo's own note that a GPU job only needs the host
    # driver via --nv at run time, not at build time -- the CUDA runtime
    # itself is already bundled in these conda packages.
    export CONDA_OVERRIDE_CUDA=11.8
    mamba env create -f /opt/deeppsa/environment.yaml -p /opt/conda-deeppsa
    mamba clean -afy

%environment
    export PATH=/opt/conda-deeppsa/bin:/opt/miniforge3/bin:${PATH}

%runscript
    cd /opt/deeppsa
    exec python "$@"

%apprun predict
    # PSA.py hardcodes cwd-relative paths (checkpoint at
    # PSA/FinetuneModel.pth, a data_process/mytest/ graph cache, and its
    # result.csv output), so it must run from a writable copy of
    # /opt/deeppsa. --writable-tmpfs looked like the fit (same pattern as
    # GASA in scoring.def) but isn't: its overlay is a small, RAM-backed
    # tmpfs (confirmed 16M default by actually running a real ~2200-row
    # test set, not the earlier 3-molecule smoke test -- DGL's graph cache
    # blew straight through it, "OSError: [Errno 28] No space left on
    # device" writing the *final* result.csv). /tmp, unlike /opt, is
    # already real host-backed disk inside the container by default (no
    # --writable-tmpfs needed -- apptainer bind-mounts it in), so this
    # symlinks the whole DeepPSA tree into a scratch dir under /tmp instead
    # (cp -rs: symlinks, not copies, so the ~560MB of checkpoint weights
    # cost nothing) and runs from there. New files (the graph cache,
    # result.csv) land as real files on that real disk; reads of the
    # checkpoint through the symlink are unaffected by it being read-only.
    set -eu
    if [ "$#" -ne 2 ]; then
        echo "usage: apptainer run --app predict deeppsa.sif <input.csv> <output.csv>" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    INPUT=$(readlink -f "$1")
    # Resolve OUTPUT to an absolute path too, and before the cd below --
    # confirmed by actually running this against a real dataset, not just
    # the earlier smoke test: a relative output path (the natural way to
    # call this) silently resolved against $SCRATCH instead of the
    # caller's own directory once the script cd'd there, so `cp -f
    # result.csv "$OUTPUT"` failed with a "No such file or directory" that
    # only shows up after the (potentially slow) prediction step already
    # ran to completion.
    OUTPUT=$(readlink -f "$2" 2>/dev/null) || { echo "output directory for '$2' does not exist" >&2; exit 1; }
    SCRATCH=$(mktemp -d /tmp/deeppsa-predict.XXXXXX)
    trap 'rm -rf "$SCRATCH"' EXIT
    cp -rs /opt/deeppsa/. "$SCRATCH/"
    cd "$SCRATCH"
    python PSA.py "$INPUT"
    cp -f result.csv "$OUTPUT"

%labels
    profile deeppsa
    upstream https://github.com/Zhang-Ran-0119/DeepPSA

%help
    DeepPSA container: geometric-deep-learning PROTAC synthetic-accessibility
    scorer from https://github.com/Zhang-Ran-0119/DeepPSA, run standalone --
    not part of this project's own pyproject.toml extras (see the header
    comment in this file for why).

    Build (from the repo root; slow -- several GB of conda-forge/PyPI/GitHub
    downloads. Needs the login node's internet access -- compute nodes
    reached via SLURM have none, so never run this inside an sbatch job; see
    slurm/build_deeppsa_sif.sh for a login-node background build):
        apptainer build --fakeroot apptainer/deeppsa.sif apptainer/deeppsa.def

    Run inference with the pretrained+finetuned model. Input CSV needs
    'smiles' and 'labels' columns -- DeepPSA's own data loader requires a
    labels column even for pure inference; fill it with dummy 0s if you
    don't have ground truth. No --writable-tmpfs needed (see the predict
    app's comment above in this file -- it runs from a real-disk scratch
    dir under /tmp instead); add --nv only if you want it on GPU (device is
    auto-detected via torch.cuda.is_available(), falls back to CPU
    otherwise):
        apptainer run --app predict apptainer/deeppsa.sif \
            data/deeppsa/input.csv data/deeppsa/output.csv

    Output columns: smiles, es (easy-to-synthesize score), hs
    (hard-to-synthesize score), lable_pre (predicted class -- DeepPSA's own
    spelling, not a typo introduced here).

    License: DeepPSA is released under the Academic Free Licence -- academic
    use, modification, and redistribution are permitted; unauthorised
    commercial use is not. See /opt/deeppsa/LICENSE inside the image, or
    https://github.com/Zhang-Ran-0119/DeepPSA/blob/main/LICENSE.
